ices in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool can run 'kubectl get services' in that namespace. That would give a list of services, which is more reliable than relying on Grafana. \n\nSo, using kubectl makes sense here. It's a direct way to get the services without depending on Grafana's data. If the services are present in Kubernetes but not visible in Grafana, the user can still proceed with the kubectl output. Therefore, the next tool should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command 'kubectl get services -n astronomy-shop'.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nThe `get_services` tool returned no results, which could indicate either no services are present, or the tool is not scoped to the correct namespace. To directly inspect the Kubernetes cluster's state, I will use `kubectl get services -n astronomy-shop` to list all services in the target namespace. This provides a reliable, first-principles view of the application's workload structure.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=489, prompt_tokens=1663, total_tokens=2152, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb18-3834-7683-be30-fedf3145dfd4-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1663, 'output_tokens': 489, 'total_tokens': 2152}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='199780cb-88fe-476b-8983-3fb85c895011')]]
